CHANGELOG.md:13:- `SessionStore.listFor(userId, options?)`, an optional store member that answers the summaries of a user's conversations — `sessionId`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt` and `entryCount` — most recent activity first, so the WebSocket transport can deliver a conversation list instead of only rejoining one. A store of your own may leave it out; the engine reports that absence as an empty list. A summary never carries a conversation's entries or its credentials. Page with `limit` (50 by default, 200 at most, and `0` answers nothing) and `before`, which takes the last summary you saw: the cursor carries both `updatedAt` and `sessionId`, so two conversations saved in the same millisecond cannot straddle a page boundary and go unlisted. See `docs/session-stores.md` § Listing a user's conversations.
CHANGELOG.md:18:- `MongoSessionStore.latestFor` now breaks a tie on `updatedAt` by `_id`, so it and `listFor` agree on which conversation is newest when two were saved in the same millisecond. Which of two tied conversations a resume rejoins can therefore change.
CHANGELOG.md:19:- The index to create for `MongoSessionStore` is now `{userId: 1, updatedAt: -1, _id: -1}`. The two-key form that served `latestFor` cannot satisfy `listFor`'s sort, so Mongo would sort a user's whole history on every page. `docs/session-stores.md` § Latest session has the command.
CHANGELOG.md:20:- `CollectionLike` gains an optional `find`, and `CursorLike`, `FindManyOptions` and `FindOneOptions` are now exported for anyone writing their own adapter. A collection without `find` still satisfies the type; `listFor` refuses on it rather than reporting that the user has no conversations.
CHANGELOG.md:42:- Stored assistant and thinking entries gain an optional `suppressed: true` when a tool suppressed the response and the client was shown the entry blank or not at all. A resumed session replays such an entry as `entry_skip`, so a reconnecting client never sees text a tool hid. The entry keeps its text for the model's history.
specs/hal-engine-websocket-protocol/spec.md:141:- The waiting message's model request therefore holds the earlier answer finished, never one still streaming ([validated by: hands the second run a history whose first answer is finished, never one still streaming](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L725)).
specs/hal-engine-websocket-protocol/spec.md:186:`list_sessions` is answered outside the per-session turn lock, so it can return while a user message is streaming. The frame is accepted by validation whether or not history listing is enabled; configuration determines whether the server answers it.
specs/hal-engine-websocket-protocol/spec.md:280:Notifies the client that a session entry was created on the server and will never be sent. This happens under output suppression (see [tool-responses.md](../hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md)): the server keeps the entry so the model retains context, and the client does not need it for display ([validated by: still records a suppressed reply in the session, so history keeps it](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L259)).
specs/hal-engine-websocket-protocol/spec.md:386:The list is scoped to the connection's authenticated user; the client cannot choose another user through the frame. The deployer must opt in to exposing history through the transport, and should retain it only for the consumer's configured retention period ([validated by: answers list_sessions from the authenticated session user with session summaries](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L98)).
specs/hal-engine-websocket-protocol/spec.md:388:When history is enabled, `list_conversations` answers with one `conversation_list` carrying ISO-8601 summaries and no `stream_end` ([validated by: answers list_conversations with ISO summaries when history is enabled, without stream_end](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L137)).
